[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Classify what changed before reaching for supersession

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE
TITLE: Classify what changed before reaching for supersession
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The operator reads it and turns the status, or a second genuine
abandoned-and-replaced case on a non-DECISION node forces the relation to be
extended and this rule with it.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Three different events keep getting treated as one, and only the third is
supersession. Classify before reaching for a mechanism.

A contract moved. That is a PARENT_FP hash transition, signed by the operator at
pull-request review. Not supersession.

A description went wrong or went stale. Rewrite the statement, put the retired
shape in NOTES and the reason in the commit. Not supersession. This is permitted
on every node type except an accepted DECISION, whose statement is frozen.

A thing was abandoned and different work replaced it. That is supersession.

For a DECISION the machinery exists and is unchanged: STATUS becomes superseded,
Superseded_By points at the successor, Supersedes points back.

For every other node type there is no relation, so supersession is written as
prose and the instance is logged: rewrite the statement to say the work was
abandoned, name the successor UID in the first sentence, and record it against
MECH-SLICE-CANNOT-BE-SUPERSEDED. This is deliberately worse than the DECISION
path so that instances accumulate as evidence instead of being smoothed over.

Extend Superseded_By and Supersedes to MECHANISM, SLICE, INVARIANT and SPIKE
when the second genuine instance appears. One instance is an anecdote; two is a
pattern, and two is also enough to see whether liveness means the same thing for
each type.

When it is extended, liveness is a QUERY and not a field: a node is live when
nothing points at it with Supersedes. No new enum value and no new field. DEPTH
is a design-maturity axis and a superseded node may have been fully implemented,
so folding superseded into DEPTH would destroy that distinction. The readiness
query must then skip superseded nodes, or a retired slice keeps blocking its
successor. That is the one place the extension is not free, and the reason the
trigger above waits for evidence.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Written after the machinery was hit twice in three days and read wrong once.
SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY was NOT superseded: the deliverable, an overlay keeping
strictdoc current, still exists and still ships, and what changed was the
implementation shape. Rewriting it in place was correct and the worry about a
lost transition was a misdiagnosis, because there was no transition of work to
lose. Getting that classification wrong is what makes the gap look bigger than
it is.

The trigger is set at the second instance rather than the first because the cost
of extending fell after milestone one: the grammar is generated from a typed Nix
surface now, so adding a relation role is a typed edit and a regeneration rather
than a hand-edited .sgra. A rule written before that landed would price this
wrong.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Authorized by the operator on 2026-08-27 -- "i'm fine if you make up a well
thought out supercede rule for this plan given its friction" -- and written as
open rather than accepted because they authorized the rule without reading the
text, and an accepted statement is frozen.

Scoped to this plan. Whether settled architecture under **/.sdoc/ retires the
same way is DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN's question, not this one.

Measured 2026-08-27: zero nodes in the corpus are superseded, and the grammar
declares Superseded_By and Supersedes on DECISION only. MECHANISM, SLICE and
INVARIANT carry Governed_By, Assumes and Proven_By pairs; SPIKE carries only
Governed_By and Governs.
<<<
